1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,660
You log in to a server you have never seen. No desktop. No map.

2
00:00:05,210 --> 00:00:09,135
Where are you? Where does everything live? And who do you ask when you are stuck?

3
00:00:09,685 --> 00:00:12,629
This lesson gives you the map, the moves, and the manual.

4
00:00:14,679 --> 00:00:20,930
First question on any server: where am I? Type: pwd, which means print working directory.

5
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:26,493
Slash home slash student. That is my home folder, and the prompt shows it as a tilde.

6
00:00:27,043 --> 00:00:28,814
What is here? Type: ls.

7
00:00:29,364 --> 00:00:35,124
A file, and a folder. But ls hides some things. Type: ls -a, where a means all.

8
00:00:35,674 --> 00:00:37,998
Names that start with a dot are hidden.

9
00:00:37,998 --> 00:00:39,786
The single dot is this folder,

10
00:00:39,786 --> 00:00:41,455
and two dots are its parent.

11
00:00:42,005 --> 00:00:45,568
For details, type: ls -l. That is a long listing.

12
00:00:46,118 --> 00:00:49,118
The first letter is the type: dash for a file,

13
00:00:49,118 --> 00:00:50,292
d for a directory.

14
00:00:50,292 --> 00:00:51,401
Then permissions,

15
00:00:51,401 --> 00:00:51,792
owner,

16
00:00:51,792 --> 00:00:52,119
size,

17
00:00:52,119 --> 00:00:53,032
date and name.

18
00:00:53,032 --> 00:00:55,184
Permissions get their own lesson.

19
00:00:57,234 --> 00:00:58,962
Four shortcuts make paths short.

20
00:00:59,512 --> 00:01:01,176
A dot means here, this folder.

21
00:01:01,726 --> 00:01:03,945
Two dots mean the parent, one level up.

22
00:01:04,495 --> 00:01:06,393
A tilde means your home folder.

23
00:01:06,943 --> 00:01:10,293
A dash, after CD, means the last folder you were in.

24
00:01:10,843 --> 00:01:13,426
A path that starts with a slash is absolute,

25
00:01:13,426 --> 00:01:14,835
and works from anywhere.

26
00:01:14,835 --> 00:01:16,361
Anything else is relative,

27
00:01:16,361 --> 00:01:18,181
and depends on where you stand.

28
00:01:20,231 --> 00:01:20,925
Now move.

29
00:01:20,925 --> 00:01:23,318
A path says where something is.

30
00:01:23,318 --> 00:01:25,787
Type: cd projects/cloudcart/app.

31
00:01:25,787 --> 00:01:27,639
This is a relative path,

32
00:01:27,639 --> 00:01:30,108
so it starts from where I stand.

33
00:01:30,658 --> 00:01:36,546
No output means it worked. The prompt now shows the full route. Confirm with: pwd.

34
00:01:37,096 --> 00:01:44,478
Go sideways. Type: cd ../config. Two dots go up one folder, then down into config.

35
00:01:45,028 --> 00:01:51,214
Now an absolute path. It starts with a slash. Type: cd /var/log.

36
00:01:51,764 --> 00:01:55,306
Jump back to where I was. Type: cd, space, dash.

37
00:01:55,856 --> 00:01:57,214
It prints the folder,

38
00:01:57,214 --> 00:01:58,507
and takes you there.

39
00:01:58,507 --> 00:01:59,089
Now home,

40
00:01:59,089 --> 00:01:59,995
from anywhere.

41
00:01:59,995 --> 00:02:00,577
Type: cd,

42
00:02:00,577 --> 00:02:01,999
with nothing after it.

43
00:02:02,550 --> 00:02:05,419
Last trick: let the shell type for you.

44
00:02:05,419 --> 00:02:06,081
Type: cd,

45
00:02:06,081 --> 00:02:06,523
space,

46
00:02:06,523 --> 00:02:06,964
p r o,

47
00:02:06,964 --> 00:02:08,068
then press Tab.

48
00:02:08,068 --> 00:02:08,730
Then c l,

49
00:02:08,730 --> 00:02:09,760
and Tab again.

50
00:02:10,310 --> 00:02:11,633
Tab completes names.

51
00:02:11,633 --> 00:02:12,493
It is faster,

52
00:02:12,493 --> 00:02:13,949
and it prevents typos.

53
00:02:13,949 --> 00:02:15,206
If nothing happens,

54
00:02:15,206 --> 00:02:17,521
press Tab twice to see the choices.

55
00:02:19,571 --> 00:02:21,555
Here is the map, in eight folders.

56
00:02:22,105 --> 00:02:25,860
Slash etc: configuration, as plain text files.

57
00:02:26,410 --> 00:02:29,652
Slash var: logs, caches and data that change.

58
00:02:30,202 --> 00:02:32,826
Slash usr: programs and libraries.

59
00:02:33,376 --> 00:02:35,979
Slash home: one folder for every user.

60
00:02:36,529 --> 00:02:39,473
Slash tmp: temporary files, cleared at reboot.

61
00:02:40,023 --> 00:02:45,122
Slash proc and slash dev: not real files. The kernel's live view, and devices.

62
00:02:45,672 --> 00:02:50,856
Slash bin and slash sbin: on modern Ubuntu, links into slash usr.

63
00:02:51,406 --> 00:02:54,350
Learn these eight, and any Linux server feels familiar.

64
00:02:56,400 --> 00:03:02,608
Linux has one tree. Everything starts at a single slash, called root. Type: ls /.

65
00:03:03,158 --> 00:03:03,893
No C drive,

66
00:03:03,893 --> 00:03:04,628
no D drive.

67
00:03:04,628 --> 00:03:07,032
Disks are attached inside this tree.

68
00:03:07,032 --> 00:03:09,771
These are the standard top level folders.

69
00:03:10,321 --> 00:03:16,337
Look closer at three of them. Type: ls -ld /bin /sbin /lib

70
00:03:16,467 --> 00:03:17,995
Each one is an arrow,

71
00:03:17,995 --> 00:03:19,741
pointing into slash usr.

72
00:03:19,741 --> 00:03:22,942
Modern Ubuntu keeps every program under usr,

73
00:03:22,942 --> 00:03:24,979
and the old paths are links.

74
00:03:25,529 --> 00:03:34,148
Settings live in slash etc. See which Linux this is. Type: head -3 /etc/os-release

75
00:03:34,278 --> 00:03:34,760
Ubuntu,

76
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:36,551
twenty four point oh four.

77
00:03:36,551 --> 00:03:37,791
A plain text file.

78
00:03:37,791 --> 00:03:41,510
Editing files like this is how you configure a server.

79
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:47,415
Package settings sit in folders here too. Type: ls /etc/apt

80
00:03:47,545 --> 00:03:49,659
One folder per job: sources,

81
00:03:49,659 --> 00:03:50,037
keys,

82
00:03:50,037 --> 00:03:50,943
preferences.

83
00:03:50,943 --> 00:03:51,623
Remember,

84
00:03:51,623 --> 00:03:54,115
slash etc is where to look first.

85
00:03:54,665 --> 00:03:59,017
Logs live in slash var. Type: ls /var/log

86
00:03:59,147 --> 00:04:00,692
When a service misbehaves,

87
00:04:00,692 --> 00:04:02,831
the answer is usually in a log here.

88
00:04:02,831 --> 00:04:04,673
We read them in a later lesson.

89
00:04:05,223 --> 00:04:10,983
One more. Slash proc is not a disk. Type: head -2 /proc/meminfo

90
00:04:11,113 --> 00:04:12,607
Those numbers are real,

91
00:04:12,607 --> 00:04:13,257
right now,

92
00:04:13,257 --> 00:04:14,101
in kilobytes.

93
00:04:14,101 --> 00:04:15,855
No file on disk holds them.

94
00:04:15,855 --> 00:04:17,480
The kernel makes them up,

95
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:18,259
as you read.

96
00:04:20,309 --> 00:04:24,917
Now the third question: who do I ask? Four tools, from fastest to deepest.

97
00:04:25,467 --> 00:04:28,667
Type, and which: what is this command, and where does it live?

98
00:04:29,217 --> 00:04:32,161
Dash dash help: a short summary of the options.

99
00:04:32,711 --> 00:04:35,335
Man: the full manual, split into sections.

100
00:04:35,885 --> 00:04:39,768
Apropos: search the manuals by topic, when you forget the name.

101
00:04:40,318 --> 00:04:43,006
Start small. Go deeper only when you need to.

102
00:04:45,056 --> 00:04:48,213
Start with what a command is. Type: type cd.

103
00:04:48,763 --> 00:04:54,097
A shell builtin. It lives inside the shell itself. Compare. Type: type cp.

104
00:04:54,647 --> 00:05:02,647
A real program, in slash usr slash bin. Now quick help. Type: ls --help | head -8

105
00:05:02,777 --> 00:05:07,427
Almost every command has this. Pipe it into head, so the screen does not flood.

106
00:05:07,977 --> 00:05:10,516
For the full manual, type: man ls.

107
00:05:11,066 --> 00:05:16,250
This is a pager. Space scrolls a page. Slash, then a word, searches. And q quits.

108
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:18,516
Read three parts: the name,

109
00:05:18,516 --> 00:05:19,342
the synopsis,

110
00:05:19,342 --> 00:05:20,867
and the option you need.

111
00:05:20,867 --> 00:05:22,773
Square brackets mean optional.

112
00:05:23,323 --> 00:05:26,459
Manuals have sections. Type: whatis passwd.

113
00:05:27,009 --> 00:05:28,684
Passwd appears twice.

114
00:05:28,684 --> 00:05:30,836
Section one is the command.

115
00:05:30,836 --> 00:05:33,388
Section five is the file format.

116
00:05:33,388 --> 00:05:34,903
Type: man 5 passwd.

117
00:05:35,453 --> 00:05:37,064
Now it describes the file,

118
00:05:37,064 --> 00:05:37,993
field by field.

119
00:05:37,993 --> 00:05:40,657
That is the page you need when you edit it.

120
00:05:40,657 --> 00:05:41,277
Q to quit.

121
00:05:41,827 --> 00:05:48,760
And when you forget the command name, search by topic. Type: apropos -s1 'copy files'

122
00:05:48,890 --> 00:05:50,058
Copy a file: cp.

123
00:05:50,058 --> 00:05:52,248
The result tells you the name,

124
00:05:52,248 --> 00:05:53,416
and the section.

125
00:05:53,416 --> 00:05:55,461
Then man cp has the details.

126
00:05:57,511 --> 00:05:58,834
Four myths to clear up.

127
00:05:59,384 --> 00:06:04,802
Myth one: Linux has drive letters. No. One tree, and disks are attached inside it.

128
00:06:05,352 --> 00:06:08,067
Myth two: man pages are only for experts.

129
00:06:08,067 --> 00:06:08,995
Read the name,

130
00:06:08,995 --> 00:06:09,855
the synopsis,

131
00:06:09,855 --> 00:06:11,710
then search for your option.

132
00:06:12,260 --> 00:06:14,908
Myth three: run commands from anywhere.

133
00:06:14,908 --> 00:06:17,489
Many commands depend on where you are,

134
00:06:17,489 --> 00:06:18,916
so check P W D first.

135
00:06:19,466 --> 00:06:23,437
Myth four: slash bin and slash usr slash bin are different.

136
00:06:23,437 --> 00:06:24,582
On modern Ubuntu,

137
00:06:24,582 --> 00:06:26,399
one is a link to the other.

138
00:06:26,949 --> 00:06:28,037
Check, then act.

139
00:06:30,087 --> 00:06:32,412
Here is how you check your own understanding.

140
00:06:32,962 --> 00:06:35,096
Say where you are, with P W D.

141
00:06:35,646 --> 00:06:38,867
Move with relative, absolute, parent and home paths.

142
00:06:39,417 --> 00:06:42,105
Say what each of the eight top level folders holds.

143
00:06:42,655 --> 00:06:44,831
Tell a builtin from a program, with type.

144
00:06:45,381 --> 00:06:48,005
Find help with dash dash help, and with man.

145
00:06:48,555 --> 00:06:51,520
Choose the right manual section for a file format.

146
00:06:52,070 --> 00:06:53,692
To pass, you need all six.

147
00:06:55,742 --> 00:06:56,347
Your turn,

148
00:06:56,347 --> 00:06:58,343
in the virtual machine you built.

149
00:06:58,343 --> 00:06:59,976
Everything here only reads,

150
00:06:59,976 --> 00:07:01,246
so nothing can break.

151
00:07:01,796 --> 00:07:06,788
One: run P W D and ls -a in your home folder. Write down what you see.

152
00:07:07,338 --> 00:07:11,249
Two: move to slash var slash log with an absolute path,

153
00:07:11,249 --> 00:07:12,529
back with CD dash,

154
00:07:12,529 --> 00:07:14,165
and home with CD alone.

155
00:07:14,715 --> 00:07:18,704
Three: list slash, and write one line about each of the eight folders.

156
00:07:19,254 --> 00:07:23,798
Four: run type on CD and on cp, and say what the difference means.

157
00:07:24,348 --> 00:07:25,372
Five: with man,

158
00:07:25,372 --> 00:07:28,171
find which command shows free disk space,

159
00:07:28,171 --> 00:07:32,199
and which section describes the file slash etc slash hosts.

160
00:07:32,749 --> 00:07:37,250
Six: use ls only, to find where the ssh client settings live.

161
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:41,107
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

162
00:07:56,657 --> 00:07:57,532
Quick check.

163
00:07:58,082 --> 00:07:59,639
Which command shows where you are?

164
00:08:00,189 --> 00:08:02,429
What does a path that starts with a slash mean?

165
00:08:02,979 --> 00:08:05,795
Which folder holds settings, and which holds logs?

166
00:08:06,345 --> 00:08:08,628
Which tool searches manuals by topic?

167
00:08:09,178 --> 00:08:15,620
Objective covered: MC04-O01.

168
00:08:16,170 --> 00:08:18,240
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

169
00:08:28,790 --> 00:08:31,094
Next lesson: files and directories.

170
00:08:31,644 --> 00:08:34,972
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
